@title What Eighteen Months of Four-Day Weeks Did to Our Support Team
@author Priscilla Adeyemi-Koh, Head of Customer Support

# Why we tried it

I read our exit interviews back to back last year, and I did not like the pattern. In 2024, annual attrition on our support team of 23 people was 21 percent. When we asked people why they were leaving, the top reason was burnout. Not pay, not the product, not the commute. Burnout.

My first instinct was to look for a smaller fix, something that would not ask the company to change how it works. But support work is a steady drip of other people's bad days, and the drip does not stop because it is Friday afternoon. People did not need a nicer break room. They needed more actual time away from the queue.

So we asked a question that felt a little reckless: what if the whole team worked four days a week, for the same pay, and customers did not notice a drop in service? We started a pilot on 2025-04-07. I was curious, honestly, and a little nervous that the answer would be no.

# How the schedule works

Everyone works Monday to Thursday, nine hours a day. That is 36 hours a week at full pay, so nobody is squeezing a forty-hour week into four days. We shortened the week rather than compressing it.

Friday is the obvious problem, because customers still write in on Fridays. We solved it with a rotating on-call group of 4 people. They cover Friday, and in exchange they take Monday off instead. The group rotates, so no one carries Fridays for long, and every person still gets a three-day weekend most of the time.

The pilot ran for eighteen months. On 2026-10-02 we ended it with a decision to make the four-day week permanent.

# What happened to the numbers

I expected the numbers to get worse, or at best stay flat, and I was prepared to defend a small decline as the price of a happier team. That is not what happened.

- Median first response time fell from 3.4 hours to 2.9 hours.
- Customer satisfaction rose from 4.3 to 4.5 out of 5.
- Annual attrition fell from 21 percent to 9 percent.
- Sick days fell by 31 percent.

The attrition number is the one I keep coming back to. We went from losing roughly one person in five to fewer than one in ten. Every person who stays is someone who already knows our product, our customers and each other, and that shows up in everything else on the list. I do not want to claim that a shorter week alone produced faster replies. But a rested team that is not constantly training new hires is plausibly a faster team, and the data points that way.

# What got harder

I would be misleading you if I made this sound easy. Three things got harder.

First, handoffs. With everyone leaving on Thursday evening, anything unfinished at the end of the day has to be passed to the Friday group in a way that makes sense to someone who was not in the conversation. Thursday evening became the most delicate hour of our week, and we had to get much more disciplined about writing things down.

Second, two enterprise customers told us they wanted guaranteed Friday phone support. That was a fair request and a fair test of the whole idea. We chose to hire 2 more people to cover it rather than quietly stretch the on-call group, because a pilot that works only by overloading four people is not a success.

Third, a few people found nine-hour days tiring in winter. Dark mornings and dark evenings make a long day feel longer. We are still talking about whether some people should have a different daily shape, and I do not have a clean answer yet.

# If you try it

I am not here to tell you every team should do this. I can only tell you what I would do again, in three pieces of advice.

1. Pilot for at least 6 months. A month is a honeymoon. You need long enough to see the winter, the busy season and the first real handoff failure.
2. Write the coverage rules before the first week. Who is on call, who they hand to, what counts as urgent, and what customers are promised. Ambiguity in week one turns into resentment by week four.
3. Measure customer metrics alongside team morale. If you track only how people feel, you will not know whether customers are paying for it. If you track only the customers, you will miss the reason you started.

We went in expecting a trade-off between a happier team and happier customers. After eighteen months, we found a lot less trade-off than we feared.
